22:28:43 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: That’s a brilliant observation, and you’ve spotted one of the most shocking mismatches in the consumer world. The short answer is that **you’re not just paying for ink; you’re paying for the packaging, the retail markup, the printer’s business model, and the technology gap between a home printer and a printing press.** Let’s break it down, because every factor stacks the deck against you. ### 1. The ink itself: tankers vs. thimbles Newsprint ink (even the colored kind) is a completely different beast from the liquid gold in your inkjet cartridge. - **Type of ink:** Newspaper presses use coldset web offset ink. It’s essentially a mix of pigment, oil, and resin that dries by being absorbed into the paper. Your home inkjet uses water-based dye or pigment inks with very different chemistries. Laser printers use plastic-based toner. The offset ink is inherently cheaper to produce. - **Scale of purchase:** A newspaper buys ink by the tanker truck or rail car—thousands of gallons at a time—at a bulk commodity price (pennies per pound). You buy a tiny cartridge containing perhaps 5–15 ml of ink, packaged in a complex plastic shell with a built-in print head, microchips, and retail blister packaging. The cartridge manufacturing and packaging costs far more than the ink inside. - **The “razor and blades” trap:** Printer manufacturers often sell the hardware at a loss and make their profit on the cartridges. Inkjet ink is one of the most expensive liquids on earth, often priced at $2,000–$8,000 per gallon when bought in these tiny consumer cartridges. In industrial quantities, offset inks might cost $10–$30 per gallon. ### 2. The printing process: speed and efficiency A newspaper web offset press is a marvel of efficiency. - It prints simultaneously on both sides of a continuous paper web that moves at 50–80 km/h, cranking out up to 90,000 full-colour copies per hour on massive rolls. A typical plant uses a few hundred dollars of ink to print tens of thousands of copies. - Ink transfer is extremely efficient. The offset process spreads a very thin, consistent film of ink, and there’s no constant cleaning/priming cycle like your home printer does every time it switches on (gobbling up a terrifying percentage of your cartridge for maintenance). - Your home printer uses a tiny motor, prints one line at a time, and typically wastes a shocking amount of ink in head-cleaning cycles just to keep the nozzles from clogging. ### 3. Coverage: your “newspaper page” dream vs. reality If you actually print a page that looks like a typical newspaper colour page, you’re not printing a full-bleed, saturated photo. Newspapers use colour mostly for headlines, charts, maps, and occasional photos that are deliberately half-toned for economy. The average ink coverage on a newspaper page is very low—often 20–30% or less. If you print an equivalent page at home using a “draft” or normal quality setting on plain paper, you’d be surprised how much less ink it actually uses than a full glossy photo. But if you tried to print a page *full of saturated, high-resolution colour*, you’re right—you’d burn through ink cartridges. The newspaper isn’t doing that kind of print either. ### 4. Paper: another world of cheap Newsprint is a thin, low-brightness, uncoated paper made from mechanical pulp. It costs the newspaper a fraction of a cent per sheet. You’re probably printing on brighter, thicker, coated or higher-quality paper that alone might cost what the newsstand copy costs. The cheap paper also helps the ink dry instantly and reduces the amount of ink required for decent print contrast. ### 5. The business model: the price you pay isn’t the price they get Even with all that efficiency, many newspapers still lose money on the physical copy at the price you pay. The cover price (say $1–$2) is a token charge. The real business is **advertising**. Advertisers cover the bulk of the production and distribution costs, because they pay for access to the reader’s eyeballs. The “dirt cheap” price is deliberately kept low to maximize circulation numbers, making advertising space more valuable. If you had to pay the full unsubsidised cost of producing that newspaper *and* then print it only once, you’d easily face a cost many times the newsstand price, even before the home-ink penalty. ### A quick back-of-the-envelope calculation A typical newspaper might cost around 15–25 cents to print, including all materials, labour, and machine time (massive operation). The ink cost per copy is maybe **1–3 cents**. Your home printer’s ink cost for a single colour page could be 15–50 cents, and that’s just for the ink, not the paper or printer depreciation. So yes, your “20x the price just on ink” is both plausible and understandable. **In summary:** you’re comparing a centuries-old industrial process operating at insane scale and buying raw materials, with a consumer product designed to extract profit from you through tiny, overpriced consumables. Next time you look at a newspaper, think of it as a subsidy-delivery vehicle for ads, printed with ink that costs less than bottled water.
